  "title": "Repairing After Emotional Mistakes",
  "content": "All parents lose their temper sometimes. What matters most is what happens afterward. Repair is the process of reconnecting with your child after a moment of disconnection. It teaches children that relationships can recover and that mistakes do not break love.\n\nRepair involves acknowledging what happened, taking responsibility, and offering reassurance. It does not require long speeches—simple, honest statements are powerful. When parents repair consistently, children learn emotional resilience and trust.\n\nThis lesson helps parents understand that repair is not a sign of weakness but a sign of strength. It models accountability, empathy, and healthy emotional expression.",
  "activities": [
    "Think of a recent moment when you became overwhelmed. Practice a repair statement such as 'I’m sorry I yelled. You didn’t deserve that. I’m working on staying calm.'",
    "Notice how your child responds when you repair after a difficult moment."
  ],
  "reflectionQuestions": [
    "How was repair handled in your childhood?",
